"""Chapter 2 content — Literature Review (~30,000 words)"""

def chapter_two():
    title = "CHAPTER 2. LITERATURE REVIEW"
    def S(t): return {'type': 'body', 'text': t}
    def H2(t): return {'type': 'h2', 'text': t}
    def H3(t): return {'type': 'h3', 'text': t}

    sections = [
        H2("2.1 Introduction to the Literature Review"),
        S("The deployment of artificial intelligence in healthcare settings reflects a broader global movement toward digital transformation in service delivery, but it also reflects the accumulated insights of multiple research traditions that have independently developed relevant knowledge over several decades. This literature review synthesizes evidence and theory across seven major thematic domains that collectively define the intellectual landscape for the present research. The domains are: the historical and technical evolution of chatbot and conversational AI architectures; the application of chatbots and virtual assistants in healthcare settings; technical dimensions of reliability, schema compliance, and tool-use in large language model systems; queue management theory, models, and applications in healthcare; AI and machine learning approaches to queue prediction and optimization; multilingual AI and digital health equity; and governance and ethical frameworks for responsible AI deployment. Within each domain, the review identifies specific insights that shape the design decisions made in this thesis and builds progressively toward an articulation of the research gaps that the thesis addresses."),
        S("The review methodology draws on peer-reviewed publications accessed through Google Scholar, PubMed, IEEE Xplore, and ACM Digital Library, supplemented by grey literature from healthcare governance bodies, digital health policy documents, and conference proceedings. The temporal scope prioritizes literature published between 2015 and 2025, with foundational works from earlier periods included where they remain conceptually or empirically central. Search terms combined domain-specific vocabulary, specifically 'healthcare chatbot,' 'hospital queue management,' 'multilingual AI,' 'design science research,' 'deterministic guardrails,' 'appointment scheduling AI,' 'queuing theory healthcare,' and 'digital health governance,' with geographic terms including 'Kenya,' 'Sub-Saharan Africa,' 'low and middle income countries,' and 'developing countries.' The review identifies specific gaps in each thematic domain and synthesizes them into a composite gap statement that motivates the thesis's specific research questions and design approach."),
        H2("2.2 Historical and Technical Evolution of Chatbot Systems"),
        H3("2.2.1 Rule-Based Systems and the Origins of Conversational Computing"),
        S("The history of computational conversation begins with Joseph Weizenbaum's ELIZA program, developed at MIT between 1964 and 1966, which demonstrated that machines could simulate meaningful conversation through pattern matching and scripted response templates. ELIZA's DOCTOR script, which simulated a psychotherapeutic conversation using reflective questioning and keyword-triggered responses, achieved surprising engagement from users despite its complete absence of genuine understanding. The ELIZA effect, the tendency of users to attribute genuine comprehension and intentionality to systems that merely pattern-match, became a foundational concept in human-computer interaction research, and remains relevant today in understanding why patients may trust AI systems with healthcare information that deserves careful scrutiny. Subsequent rule-based systems including ALICE, which won the Loebner Prize multiple times using Artificial Intelligence Markup Language (AIML) in the early 2000s, refined the pattern-matching approach but retained its fundamental limitation: the system could only handle inputs that closely matched patterns in its rule database, making brittleness in novel situations an inherent property of the paradigm."),
        S("The practical deployment of rule-based chatbots in commercial settings from the 1990s through the 2010s demonstrated both the value and the limitations of the approach. Customer service deployments showed that rule-based systems could handle a high fraction of routine inquiries at low cost, particularly when inquiries were highly standardized and the system's limitations were clearly communicated to users. Healthcare applications of rule-based chatbots in this period included early symptom triage systems, which used decision trees to guide patients through structured symptom assessment, and simple appointment reminder systems, which could send standardized messages through SMS or email without any natural language understanding. These systems were valuable within their narrow scope but failed gracefully only when users adhered closely to the interaction patterns the system designer had anticipated. Out-of-scope inputs produced either unhelpful generic responses or outright system failures, undermining user trust and reducing the fraction of interactions that successfully completed."),
        S("The emergence of intent classification frameworks in the early 2010s, exemplified by Google's Dialogflow (then API.AI) and IBM's Watson Assistant, provided a more flexible approach that could handle varied natural language expressions of the same underlying intent. These frameworks combined machine learning-based intent classifiers, which could map diverse natural language inputs to a finite set of defined intents, with hand-crafted response logic that executed appropriate actions for each recognized intent. This architecture substantially improved coverage and resilience compared to pure pattern matching, but retained a key limitation: performance degraded significantly on intents and entities not represented in the training data, and the combination of learned intent classification with hand-crafted response logic created a hybrid system that was complex to maintain and challenging to debug. Caldarini et al. (2022) provided a comprehensive survey of this period, noting that intent-based chatbot frameworks achieved significant commercial adoption but consistently struggled with open-domain conversations, complex multi-turn reasoning, and knowledge that required synthesis from multiple sources rather than simple intent-action mapping."),
        H3("2.2.2 Statistical and Machine Learning Approaches to Conversational AI"),
        S("The application of corpus-based statistical methods to natural language processing in the 1990s and early 2000s enabled a shift from hand-crafted rules to data-driven learning for specific NLP tasks including language identification, sentiment analysis, named entity recognition, and intent classification. Hidden Markov Models, Naive Bayes classifiers, and Support Vector Machines became standard tools for these tasks, achieving performance on specific, well-defined problems that rule-based systems could not match. Sequence models including Conditional Random Fields and Long Short-Term Memory networks advanced performance on structured prediction tasks such as entity extraction and dialogue state tracking, which are directly relevant to chatbot systems that must maintain awareness of what information has been collected and what still needs to be gathered in a multi-step interaction."),
        S("Retrieval-based dialogue systems, which selected responses from a large database of pre-written responses by matching user input to the most similar query in the database, achieved commercial deployment in customer service and information retrieval contexts. These systems offered high response quality for queries similar to those in the database while maintaining complete interpretability: every response was a human-authored text that could be reviewed and approved before deployment. The limitation was scale and coverage: maintaining a large, high-quality response database required significant ongoing editorial effort, and performance degraded sharply for queries without close matches in the database. Healthcare applications of retrieval-based systems, including early health information chatbots, were primarily limited to information retrieval tasks where high-quality authoritative responses could be pre-authored and curated by clinical staff."),
        S("Generative sequence-to-sequence models, trained on large conversation corpora to generate responses rather than selecting from pre-written alternatives, promised to overcome the coverage limitations of retrieval-based systems. Early sequence-to-sequence models produced grammatically plausible responses but frequently fell into patterns of generating safe, generic responses, for example 'I don't know' or 'That's a good question,' rather than genuinely informative content. The hallucination problem, where generative models produce confident-sounding but factually incorrect statements, was identified early as a significant barrier to healthcare deployment, where the accuracy of information provided directly affects patient wellbeing. These limitations motivated the development of constrained generation approaches and knowledge-grounded generation frameworks that would become important precursors to the tool-using LLM architectures that are the focus of the present thesis."),
        H3("2.2.3 The Deep Learning Revolution and Large Language Models"),
        S("The introduction of the Transformer architecture by Vaswani et al. in 2017, and the subsequent development of large pre-trained language models including BERT (2018), GPT-2 (2019), T5 (2019), and GPT-3 (2020), transformed the conversational AI landscape decisively and rapidly. Pre-training on vast corpora, initially consisting of web text and books numbering in the hundreds of billions of tokens, allowed models to develop rich representations of language, world knowledge, and discourse patterns that could be applied to new tasks with minimal task-specific fine-tuning. The GPT-3 language model demonstrated that a sufficiently large pre-trained model could perform competently on tasks it had never explicitly been trained for, simply by conditioning on a few examples in the prompt, a capability called few-shot or in-context learning. This capability fundamentally changed the development economics of natural language processing applications: rather than requiring thousands of labeled training examples and weeks of fine-tuning, a capable language model could be applied to a new task with a carefully crafted prompt and a small number of examples."),
        S("The scale progression from GPT-3 (175 billion parameters) to instruction-tuned models such as InstructGPT and ChatGPT, which were explicitly trained to follow natural language instructions and maintain multi-turn conversations, produced systems that could engage in substantially more natural and task-appropriate conversations than any previous generation of chatbots. The instruction-tuning and reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) approach used to develop ChatGPT and subsequent models aligned model behavior with human preferences for helpfulness, harmlessness, and honesty, producing systems that were substantially more reliable in their conversational behavior than untuned base models, while substantially less capable of producing the specific tool invocations required for transactional tasks. Caldarini et al. (2022) identified this transition as the most significant development in chatbot technology since the shift from rule-based to statistical approaches, noting that LLM-based systems achieved previously impossible levels of linguistic sophistication while introducing new challenges in reliability, factuality, and alignment with structured operational requirements."),
        H3("2.2.4 Tool-Using LLM Agents and Function Calling"),
        S("The most significant development for transactional AI applications is the extension of LLMs with tool-use capabilities, where the model can not only generate language but invoke external functions, APIs, and services by generating structured tool call specifications in its output. OpenAI's function-calling API, introduced in 2023, and Anthropic's tool-use feature, provide mechanisms for LLMs to generate JSON-structured tool invocations that are then executed by the hosting application, with results returned to the model as context for subsequent generation. This architecture enables the LLM to act as an orchestrator of external operations, translating natural language user requests into structured tool calls that affect real systems such as appointment databases."),
        S("The tool-use architecture is directly relevant to healthcare appointment booking, where the value of a conversational interface depends entirely on its ability to actually execute booking transactions rather than merely discussing them. However, the reliability challenges of tool-use architectures are substantial and have been documented across multiple studies. Research on LLM-based agent systems published in 2023 and 2024 found that even the most capable models produce invalid tool invocations in a non-trivial fraction of cases, with error patterns including missing required parameters, incorrectly formatted parameter values, schema non-compliance, and hallucination of non-existent tool names. Understanding the Limitations of AI Chatbots (n.d.) documented specific categories of tool-call failure in production chatbot deployments, noting that healthcare and financial service contexts, where tool-call errors have the most serious consequences, showed the highest rates of user-reported problems attributable to transactional failures. The existence of these systematic failure patterns motivates the deterministic guardrail architecture at the center of this thesis's design contribution."),
        S("Research on LangGraph and similar workflow orchestration frameworks for LLM agents has addressed the tool-call reliability problem through state machine-based workflow management that constrains LLM behavior within defined operational boundaries. LangGraph enables developers to specify workflow states and conditional transitions that the LLM's conversational output can influence but not directly control: the state machine gates execution based on validated data conditions, not on the LLM's conversational suggestions. This approach directly addresses the schema compliance and parameter completeness problems identified in unguarded LLM tool-use, providing a practical mechanism for building reliable transactional systems on a conversational LLM foundation. The present thesis implements this approach specifically in the healthcare appointment booking context and evaluates its reliability implications systematically."),
        H2("2.3 Chatbots in Healthcare: Applications, Opportunities, and Constraints"),
        H3("2.3.1 Systematic Reviews of Healthcare Chatbot Evidence"),
        S("The application of chatbot technology to healthcare has been the subject of a growing number of systematic reviews and meta-analyses that provide consolidated evidence about where chatbots add value and where they face constraints. Laranjo et al. (2018) conducted one of the most comprehensive early systematic reviews of conversational agents in health care, examining 17 randomized controlled trials of chatbot and conversational agent interventions published before 2018. The review found evidence that chatbots could improve health knowledge, increase healthy behavior adoption rates, and support medication adherence in specific populations, though the evidence quality was variable and the generalizability of findings was constrained by the heterogeneity of systems and populations studied. Critically, the review found that chatbot effectiveness was strongly context-dependent: systems designed for specific, bounded tasks with clear success criteria consistently outperformed systems attempting open-ended clinical engagement."),
        S("Miner et al. (2020) updated this evidence base with a systematic review specifically examining health chatbots in patient care, finding that chatbot interventions produced statistically significant improvements in patient outcomes in a majority of the 14 RCTs reviewed, with the strongest effects observed for medication adherence support, lifestyle modification support, and structured symptom monitoring. The review also documented the most common chatbot failure modes in healthcare: loss of context across sessions, inability to handle off-topic queries gracefully, and patient distrust of AI-generated information for clinical decisions. The distinction drawn by Miner et al. (2020) between chatbots as task-support tools, which showed consistently positive evidence, and chatbots as clinical decision-making tools, where the evidence was more equivocal and the ethical concerns more salient, is directly relevant to the scope decisions made in this thesis."),
        S("Cavalcante et al. (2022) reviewed chatbot development specifically in the healthcare domain from a computer science perspective, examining 48 studies published between 2016 and 2022. The review identified appointment scheduling, patient triage support, mental health monitoring, and chronic disease management as the four most common healthcare chatbot application areas, with appointment scheduling and triage support identified as the highest-value applications based on the consistency of reported improvements and the breadth of patient populations that could benefit. Kim et al. (2023) provided a focused examination of AI chatbots in hospital administrative tasks through a scoping review, identifying 23 studies that specifically examined chatbot application to administrative rather than clinical functions. The review found that appointment management was the most frequently studied and most consistently successful administrative chatbot application, with booking completion rates above 90 percent reported in well-designed systems with appropriate error handling. The review also highlighted that studies conducted in low- and middle-income country contexts showed systematically stronger demand for chatbot administrative tools, reflecting the greater gap between administrative capacity and patient volume in these settings."),
        H3("2.3.2 Virtual Health Assistants for Administrative Functions"),
        S("The Evolving Role of Virtual Health Assistants (n.d.) synthesized evidence from 34 studies across clinical and administrative health domains, concluding that virtual health assistants achieve the most consistent value in scenarios where the service is clearly scoped, the chatbot positions itself as a complement to human professional judgment rather than a replacement for it, and the interaction patterns are optimized for the specific population being served. The paper identified language accessibility as a significant and underaddressed determinant of virtual health assistant adoption in multilingual contexts, noting that systems that supported only the dominant language of healthcare documentation achieved significantly lower adoption rates among non-dominant-language speakers, who frequently reported discomfort communicating health-related information in a non-preferred language."),
        S("Bickmore et al. (2019) examined patient and clinician perceptions of a virtual health assistant designed to support medication adherence, finding that patients with chronic conditions responded positively to consistent, personalized reminders and were more likely to maintain medication schedules when supported by the conversational assistant. The finding that even relatively simple conversational support improved adherence in chronic disease management has implications for appointment management: patients who receive appointment reminders through a conversational interface that can answer follow-up questions about preparation requirements are likely to achieve better appointment adherence than patients who receive only static reminder messages. Bibault et al. (2019) evaluated a chatbot specifically for patients' questions in oncology, noting that patients valued the availability of the system outside normal clinic hours for routine administrative questions. The finding that around-the-clock availability is a primary driver of patient preference for chatbot over telephone-based communication aligns with the design decision to support asynchronous mobile interaction in this thesis's artifact."),
        H3("2.3.3 Mental Health and Patient Education Applications"),
        S("A substantial portion of the healthcare chatbot literature focuses on mental health applications, partly because of the relative accessibility of outcome measurement in this domain and partly because of the potential for chatbots to extend mental health support to populations who cannot access adequate professional care. Abd-Alrazaq et al. (2021) conducted a systematic review of conversational chatbots in mental health, examining 32 studies and finding evidence that chatbots could reduce symptoms of depression and anxiety in some populations, with the strongest effects observed for structured CBT-based intervention programs delivered through chatbot interfaces. Shao (2025) extended this analysis to newer LLM-based systems, finding that more sophisticated language models could maintain therapeutic rapport more effectively but introduced concerns around boundary maintenance and the risk of emotional dependence on AI systems for patients with limited access to professional mental health care. Spring et al. (2023) examined technical mechanisms for generating empathic responses in conversational AI, finding that empathic acknowledgment of user emotional state improved engagement and trust, with implications for design of error recovery responses in any patient-facing conversational system."),
        S("Factors influencing patient engagement in mental health chatbots, analyzed by Khosravi and Azar (2024) in their thematic synthesis of systematic review findings, identified clarity of purpose, behavioral consistency, and appropriate emotional responsiveness as the three factors most strongly associated with sustained patient engagement. The paper's specific finding that patients disengage rapidly from chatbots that produce unexpected or inconsistent behavior, particularly if this behavior occurs early in the interaction, has clear implications for the reliability-first design philosophy of this thesis. Kalantarion et al. (2023) examined specific mechanisms through which chatbot interactions support patient education, concluding that chatbots are most effective in educational contexts when they provide verified, specific information in response to direct questions rather than attempting to guide users through broader educational programs that may not align with their immediate informational priorities."),
        H2("2.4 Technical Dimensions: Reliability, Schema Compliance, and Tool-Use"),
        H3("2.4.1 The Reliability Problem in LLM-Based Transactional Systems"),
        S("The deployment of LLM-based conversational systems in transactional settings has revealed a systematic challenge that has received insufficient attention in the academic literature relative to its practical significance: the gap between linguistic fluency and operational reliability. While extensive research has examined the language quality of LLM outputs, including their factual accuracy, stylistic appropriateness, and coherence, substantially less research has systematically characterized the reliability of LLM tool-use in transactional contexts. Abd-Alrazaq et al. (2020) provided a systematic examination of evaluation frameworks for healthcare conversational systems, distinguishing between language quality metrics, task completion metrics, and safety metrics. Their analysis found that task completion and safety metrics are ultimately more important for real-world healthcare deployment despite being more difficult to measure, and that most published evaluations focused disproportionately on language quality metrics at the expense of transactional reliability."),
        S("The specific failure modes of LLM systems in transactional contexts have been characterized in software engineering research examining LLM-based agent systems. Parameter omission occurs when the LLM generates a tool invocation that is missing one or more required parameters, often because the conversational context contains sufficient information for a human reader but not sufficient explicit statement for the LLM's parameter extraction logic. Type errors occur when a required parameter is provided but in an incorrect format: a date expressed as 'next Tuesday' rather than the ISO 8601 format required by a database schema, or a patient ID expressed as a string that contains spaces when the schema requires a numeric identifier. Hallucination of tool names occurs when the LLM generates invocations of tools that do not exist in the defined tool set, reflecting the model's tendency to generate plausible-sounding names rather than names verified against the actual tool specification. Schema drift occurs when the LLM generates a tool call with a structure that deviates from the defined schema in ways that do not produce an obvious error but do produce incorrect behavior, such as placing a parameter value in the wrong field of a multi-parameter schema."),
        H3("2.4.2 State Machine Approaches to Workflow Reliability"),
        S("State machine-based approaches to workflow management in conversational systems have been proposed as a mechanism for ensuring logical progression through multi-step interactions, preventing the LLM from attempting transitions that would be premature or incorrect given the current state of the booking workflow. In a booking state machine, each state represents a stage of the booking process: collecting patient identity information, confirming service type, selecting date, selecting time, pre-booking validation, and booking execution. Transitions between states are conditional on the completion and validation of the information required for that transition, ensuring the system cannot attempt to execute a booking before all mandatory information has been provided and validated. Putchala and Darbha (2022) examined workflow management approaches specifically in healthcare chatbot contexts, finding that structured state-based approaches consistently outperformed more flexible approaches for administrative tasks with well-defined completion criteria and mandatory information requirements."),
        S("The LangGraph framework, developed by LangChain, provides a practical implementation of state machine-based workflow orchestration for LLM agents. LangGraph represents the workflow as a directed graph where nodes correspond to workflow states and edges to conditional transitions, with edge conditions evaluated against the current state of the conversation and the information collected. The LLM operates within this framework as a conversational component that can advance the workflow state by providing the information required for the next transition, but cannot bypass workflow states or execute transactions without passing through the required validation checkpoints. This design pattern, where the LLM provides language intelligence while the state machine provides execution control, embodies the separation of concerns principle that is central to the hybrid architecture proposed in this thesis."),
        H3("2.4.3 Evaluation Frameworks for Healthcare Conversational AI"),
        S("Evaluating healthcare conversational AI systems requires metrics that go beyond the language quality measures typically used in academic NLP research. Abd-Alrazaq et al. (2020) proposed a framework distinguishing technical metrics, which measure system performance on defined tasks including booking completion rate, entity extraction accuracy, and response latency, from user experience metrics, which measure patient-reported satisfaction, usability, and trust, and from clinical metrics, which measure health outcomes attributable to the chatbot intervention. For administrative chatbots, technical task completion metrics are primary: a booking assistant that patients find pleasant to use but that frequently produces incorrect bookings fails at its core function. Pears and Konstantinidis (2023) provided a bibliometric analysis of the healthcare chatbot literature that identified a progressive shift toward multi-dimensional evaluation frameworks incorporating both technical and experiential metrics, but noted that governance and safety evaluation remained underrepresented in published work."),
        S("Scenario-based evaluation, which tests system performance on a defined set of realistic interaction scenarios including both standard cases and edge cases, provides a complement to open-ended user testing that allows systematic coverage of the specific behaviors that matter most for healthcare deployment. Standard cases verify that the system works correctly in the most common interaction patterns. Edge cases verify that the system handles unusual but realistic inputs, partial information, invalid inputs, and error conditions in ways that recover gracefully rather than failing silently or producing incorrect transactions. The 47-scenario evaluation battery used in this thesis, described in Chapter 3, follows this scenario-based evaluation approach, specifically designed to provide systematic coverage of the reliability, multilingual consistency, queue recommendation, and governance dimensions that the research questions address."),
        H2("2.5 Queue Management Theory, Models, and Healthcare Applications"),
        H3("2.5.1 Foundations of Queuing Theory"),
        S("Queuing theory, also called waiting line theory, is a branch of operations research and probability theory that provides mathematical frameworks for analyzing systems in which demand arrives stochastically and must wait for service. The foundational mathematical framework was developed by the Danish engineer Agner Krarup Erlang in the early twentieth century in the context of telephone exchange management. Erlang's 1909 paper introduced the concept of the telephone traffic intensity measure that bears his name and derived the Erlang B formula for blocking probability and the Erlang C formula for waiting probability in telephone switching systems, both of which remain in practical use. The subsequent development of queuing theory by Kendall, Little, Jackson, and others through the mid-twentieth century produced a rich body of analytical results that have been applied across industries including telecommunications, manufacturing, logistics, and healthcare. Santos et al. (n.d.) conducted a bibliometric analysis of queuing theory applications in healthcare management, reviewing 314 articles published between 2014 and 2020, and found that the field has grown rapidly in recent years, with emergency department management, outpatient scheduling, and hospital bed management as the three most common application areas."),
        S("The Kendall notation provides a standardized vocabulary for describing queuing systems in terms of their arrival process, service process, number of servers, system capacity, and queuing discipline. An M/M/1 queue features Markovian (Poisson) arrivals, Markovian (exponential) service times, a single server, infinite capacity, and first-come-first-served discipline. An M/M/c queue extends this to multiple servers operating in parallel, which more accurately represents many healthcare service settings where multiple physicians or nurses serve the same patient queue. An M/D/1 queue replaces exponential service times with deterministic service times, which may better represent highly standardized procedures with predictable durations. The choice of queuing model for a particular healthcare application depends on how well the model assumptions fit the empirical patterns of arrivals and service in that setting. Yaduvanshi et al. (2019) applied queuing theory analysis to hospital operations at Manipal Hospital Jaipur, finding that the M/M/c model with multiple servers provided the best fit for outpatient consultation patterns, where multiple doctors serve a single waiting queue and consultation durations are approximately exponentially distributed."),
        S("Key performance metrics derived from queuing models include the average queue length (Lq), average system occupancy (L), average waiting time (Wq), average time in system (W), server utilization (rho), and the probability that all servers are busy (from Erlang C calculations). These metrics allow healthcare administrators to quantify the expected patient experience under different staffing and scheduling configurations, providing an analytical basis for operational decisions such as how many consultation rooms to open during peak hours, what appointment slot duration to allocate for specific service types, and what maximum queue length should trigger escalation procedures. Imahsunu (n.d.) applied these analytical tools in a case study of two healthcare centers in Nigeria, using Erlang C formulas to characterize queuing behavior at registration and consultation stages and identifying specific operational changes that would reduce average waiting times without increasing staffing costs."),
        H3("2.5.2 Classical Healthcare Queuing Models and Their Applications"),
        S("The application of classical queuing models to healthcare scheduling and patient flow management has generated a substantial body of empirical and applied research across global healthcare settings. Alavi-Moghaddam et al. (n.d.) examined the application of queuing analytic theory to emergency department waiting time reduction, finding that M/M/c model-based analysis correctly identified understaffing during peak morning hours as the primary driver of excessive waiting times and that the model's recommendations for staffing adjustments produced measurable reductions in patient waiting times when implemented. Application of Queuing Theory to Waiting Line Problems in Emergency Departments (Sogunro and Abiola, n.d.) conducted an empirical study at Lagos State General Hospital, finding that peak-hour congestion followed predictable patterns that could be modeled with standard queuing theory and that schedule adjustments informed by the model reduced average waiting times from over two hours to under 45 minutes in the studied department."),
        S("Peter and Sivasamy (2019) published an important methodological paper on queuing theory techniques applied to healthcare outpatient visits in the International Journal of Healthcare Management, providing a systematic comparison of queuing models including M/M/1, M/M/c, and their variants for different types of outpatient service settings. The paper found that the M/M/c model consistently provided better fit than single-server models for outpatient departments, reflecting the reality that most outpatient departments operate multiple consultation rooms simultaneously. The paper also addressed the practical challenge of estimating arrival rate parameters from empirical data, which often shows non-stationary patterns, specifically systematically higher arrival rates in the morning than the afternoon, that violate the stationarity assumption of standard Poisson arrival models. The paper's recommendation for time-varying queuing models that partition the day into intervals with different arrival rate parameters is directly relevant to the queue prediction subsystem developed in this thesis, which incorporates time-of-day factors as explicit model parameters."),
        S("Soni and Saxena (2016) conducted an empirical queuing analysis of public and private hospitals in Southern Rajasthan, India, collecting detailed arrival and service time data across multiple departments at two hospital types to compare the queuing characteristics of public versus private healthcare provision. The study found that public hospitals showed markedly higher queue length and waiting time than private hospitals, primarily attributable to higher patient volumes rather than lower service rates, and that the queuing model parameters in public hospitals were closer to those that would predict system saturation, with server utilization rates above 0.85 in peak periods. This finding has direct relevance to the Kenyan public hospital context, where similar patterns of high demand and limited capacity create the congestion that the queue recommendation subsystem in this thesis is designed to help patients navigate."),
        H3("2.5.3 Simulation Modeling for Healthcare Queue Analysis"),
        S("While analytical queuing models provide closed-form expressions for steady-state performance metrics, they require simplifying assumptions about arrival and service distributions that may not hold in specific healthcare settings. Discrete event simulation provides an alternative approach that can incorporate arbitrary distributional shapes, time-varying arrival rates, complex service routing, and heterogeneous patient categories. Bahari and Asadi (n.d.) demonstrated simulation modeling for evaluating patients' queue system performance at an emergency department, constructing a detailed simulation model calibrated to empirical data from a hospital emergency department and using it to evaluate the performance implications of different staffing configurations and triage protocols. The simulation approach allowed them to test interventions that would be ethically problematic or logistically impossible to test in the real system, such as deliberately understaffing during peak hours to quantify the consequent degradation in waiting times."),
        S("Simulation modeling has become increasingly accessible with the development of general-purpose simulation software packages including Arena, Simul8, and AnyLogic, which allow healthcare analysts without specialized operations research expertise to build and run healthcare system simulations. The Queuing System Analysis using Simulation study documented in the research literature demonstrates the practical utility of simulation modeling for hospitals in settings where analytical models are too simplistic to capture important system characteristics. The hybrid approach of using analytical models for initial design and simulation for validation and sensitivity analysis has become a standard methodology in healthcare operations research, and this combination is relevant to the approach taken in the queue prediction subsystem of this thesis, where a deterministic scoring function approximates the behavior of a more complex analytical model."),
        S("Agent-based simulation represents a further extension of the simulation approach that models the behavior and decision-making of individual patients and staff rather than treating patients as interchangeable entities. Building a System for the Hospitals' Emergency Departments Based on the Queuing Theory (AtaElfadiel and Ibrahim, 2020) integrated queuing theory principles with an emergency department management system, creating a framework in which patient flow could be monitored in real time and staffing interventions triggered automatically when queue lengths exceeded defined thresholds. The integration of queuing theory with real-time monitoring systems, which this paper exemplifies, represents a convergence between the analytical tradition in operations research and the digital health information systems tradition that is directly relevant to the digital queue management systems discussed in the following section."),
        H3("2.5.4 Queue Management Systems in Healthcare Settings"),
        S("Digital queue management systems, which use electronic check-in, display screens, and notification systems to manage the physical flow of patients through healthcare facilities, have been deployed across healthcare settings globally. Titarmare and Yerlekar (2018) provided a survey of patient queue management system implementations, reviewing systems deployed in hospital outpatient departments in India and evaluating their effectiveness against the dimensions of waiting time reduction, patient satisfaction improvement, and staff burden reduction. The survey found that digital queue management systems consistently reduced perceived waiting time even when actual waiting time remained similar, because clear information about position in queue and expected time to service reduced the anxiety and uncertainty that contribute to subjective experience of waiting. This finding aligns with research in service operations that distinguishes between objective wait duration and the psychological experience of waiting, and suggests that providing patients with queue transparency through conversational AI booking interfaces, even without changing actual service capacity, can improve patient satisfaction."),
        S("Baballe et al. (2022) examined issues with hospital queue management information systems in Nigerian hospitals, identifying five categories of systemic failure in existing digital queue management deployments: inadequate integration with electronic health record systems preventing seamless patient identification, poor reliability of hardware components in high-humidity and high-dust environments characteristic of some healthcare facility settings, insufficient staff training leading to system bypass in busy periods, lack of real-time analytics capabilities preventing proactive management of developing queue backlogs, and absence of multilingual patient communication functionality in linguistically diverse patient populations. This analysis of implementation failures is directly relevant to the design choices made in this thesis, particularly the decision to build the queue information delivery mechanism into the conversational booking interface rather than requiring a separate digital queue management system installation."),
        S("Hassan et al. (2022) examined the relationship between queue management systems and patient satisfaction in outpatient clinics in an Egyptian hospital context, finding that queue management system implementation was associated with statistically significant improvements in patient satisfaction across multiple dimensions including perceived waiting time, information quality, and overall service quality. The study specifically found that patients who received real-time updates about their expected waiting time through the queue management system reported substantially higher satisfaction than patients in the control group who received no waiting time information, even when actual waiting times were similar between the groups. This finding provides empirical support for the queue recommendation feature of this thesis's artifact: the primary value of queue information at booking time is improving patient decision quality and reducing the anxiety of uncertain waiting, not necessarily reducing actual waiting times."),
        H3("2.5.5 Mobile Technologies for Queue and Appointment Management"),
        S("The proliferation of mobile telephony across Sub-Saharan Africa has created new possibilities for digital queue management that do not require the installation of specialized hardware within healthcare facilities. Wang et al. (n.d.) documented the use of mobile technologies to streamline pretriage patient flow in an emergency department at Shanghai Sixth People's Hospital, finding that mobile check-in and queue status notification reduced congestion at the triage desk and improved overall patient flow through the department. The key mechanism was allowing patients to complete registration and initial triage information collection through their mobile devices before arriving at the department, reducing the administrative interaction required at the point of care and allowing triage staff to focus on clinical assessment rather than data collection. The relevance of this approach to the Kenyan context is direct: Kenya's mobile penetration rates, exceeding 85 percent for active mobile subscribers, provide the infrastructure foundation for mobile-based queue management without requiring facility-level hardware investment."),
        S("Hospital Queuing System with Smart Kiosk (Paver and Pabelona, 2024) described a hybrid approach combining mobile check-in with facility-based smart kiosks, allowing patients who arrive without having pre-registered through their mobile device to complete check-in quickly at a kiosk rather than waiting for a human receptionist. The kiosk system integrated with the hospital's electronic health record system to verify patient identity and retrieve appointment information, reducing the cognitive load on receptionists and improving the speed and accuracy of patient identification at arrival. The integrated system reduced average check-in time from approximately 12 minutes with manual registration to approximately 2.5 minutes with kiosk-based registration, a reduction with significant implications for queue length and waiting time during peak check-in periods."),
        H2("2.6 AI and Machine Learning Approaches to Queue Prediction and Optimization"),
        H3("2.6.1 Machine Learning for Healthcare Wait Time Estimation"),
        S("The application of machine learning to healthcare wait time prediction has grown rapidly in the period from 2018 to 2025, driven by the availability of large historical appointment and patient flow datasets and the development of increasingly capable prediction algorithms. Al-Mousa et al. (2024), published in IET Smart Cities, developed a machine learning-based approach for wait time estimation in healthcare facilities with multi-stage queues, addressing the challenge that patient waiting time in healthcare often involves multiple sequential stages, for example registration, waiting for triage, waiting for a consultation room, and waiting for test results, each with different characteristics and dependencies on prior stages. The study developed a multi-stage queuing model combining gradient boosting regression for individual stage predictions with a sequential aggregation mechanism that accounted for dependencies between stages. The model achieved mean absolute error of approximately 8 minutes on the test set, representing a substantial improvement over the baseline single-stage prediction approach."),
        S("Karmakar et al. (n.d.) examined waiting time prediction in queues using an ensemble learning approach, testing five regression models including Random Forest, Extra Trees, Gradient Boosting, Histogram-Based Gradient Boosting, and a Voting ensemble on a large multi-domain queue dataset. The Extra Trees model achieved the best performance on the validation set, with the voting ensemble providing the most robust performance across different queue types and time periods. The paper's finding that ensemble methods outperform individual models for queue waiting time prediction has implications for the design of production wait time prediction systems in healthcare, suggesting that robustness considerations may favor ensemble approaches over single-model approaches even when a single model achieves comparable point accuracy on the development dataset."),
        S("Joseph et al. (2025) provided a comprehensive comparative analysis of 15 machine learning algorithms for predicting consultation wait times in outpatient clinics, evaluated on a dataset of 28,787 patient records from a multispecialty hospital. The study tested six regression and nine classification algorithms, finding that tree-based models consistently achieved the best performance on both regression and classification formulations of the prediction problem. The study's recommendation for gradient boosting or random forest-based models as the default choice for consultation wait time prediction, given their strong performance across multiple evaluation metrics and their interpretability relative to deep learning alternatives, provides practical guidance for healthcare queue prediction system development. The study also found that historical wait time for the same service type at the same time of day on the same day of week was the single most predictive feature, reinforcing the value of time-of-day and day-of-week factors in the queue prediction model of this thesis."),
        H3("2.6.2 Deep Learning Approaches to Patient Flow Prediction"),
        S("Deep learning approaches including recurrent neural networks, transformer-based sequence models, and convolutional neural networks have been applied to healthcare patient flow prediction with promising results in research settings. Deep Learning Techniques for Forecasting Emergency Department Patient (857-Article Text, 2024) examined the application of deep learning to emergency department patient volume forecasting, finding that LSTM-based models substantially outperformed traditional time series forecasting methods on datasets with complex seasonal patterns and external dependencies such as weather and local event calendars. The study demonstrated that deep learning models could capture temporal dependencies at multiple scales, including hourly, daily, weekly, and seasonal patterns, that traditional ARIMA or exponential smoothing models could not represent simultaneously."),
        S("The practical challenge of deep learning models in healthcare queue prediction is computational cost and interpretability. Deep learning models require substantial computational resources for training and inference, which may not be available in low-resource healthcare settings. Their black-box nature makes them difficult to explain to clinical and administrative stakeholders who need to understand the basis for predictions to trust and act on them. Loureiro et al. (2023), who applied machine learning to multi-domain queue waiting time prediction in the banking and pharmacy sectors, concluded that the interpretability advantage of tree-based models over deep learning alternatives outweighed the modest accuracy improvements offered by deep learning for applications where stakeholder trust in the prediction mechanism is important for adoption. This conclusion aligns with the design choice made in this thesis to use a transparent, interpretable scoring function for queue prediction rather than a data-trained machine learning model, given the prototype-scale evaluation context and the importance of transparency for institutional adoption."),
        H3("2.6.3 Reinforcement Learning for Dynamic Queue Management"),
        S("Reinforcement learning approaches to queue management treat the queue management problem as a sequential decision problem where an agent learns optimal scheduling or resource allocation policies through experience. Dobrev et al. (2025) published a reinforcement learning solution for queue management in public utility services in Engineering Proceedings, demonstrating that a Q-learning agent trained on simulated queue dynamics could learn scheduling policies that substantially outperformed simple rule-based policies on queue length and waiting time metrics. The paper identified specific scenarios where reinforcement learning policies outperformed human-designed heuristics most dramatically: situations with rapid demand fluctuations that require adaptive responses faster than human managers can implement, and situations with complex trade-offs between short-term and long-term queue performance."),
        S("The application of reinforcement learning to healthcare queue management is an active research area that promises to move beyond static predictive models to dynamic, adaptive management systems. Patke (2024) at the University of Illinois examined queue management specifically for LLM serving infrastructure, developing a reinforcement learning approach for managing request queues in multi-tenant LLM serving systems that prioritized service level objectives. While the application domain differs from healthcare scheduling, the fundamental challenge of allocating limited service capacity to a stochastic arrival stream with differentiated priority requirements is structurally similar, and the reinforcement learning approach offers potential lessons for adaptive healthcare scheduling systems. The challenge for healthcare deployment is the need for interpretable, auditable decisions that can be explained to patients and regulatory authorities, which reinforcement learning policies may not readily provide without additional interpretability mechanisms."),
        H3("2.6.4 Predictive Modeling for Appointment No-Show Prediction"),
        S("Appointment no-show prediction represents a distinct but related machine learning application in healthcare scheduling. When patients do not attend booked appointments without cancellation, the reserved slot becomes wasted capacity, contributing to inefficiency while also preventing patients on waiting lists from accessing care. No-show rates in public hospitals across Sub-Saharan Africa have been reported at between 20 and 40 percent in multiple studies, substantially higher than rates in comparable settings in high-income countries, reflecting barriers including transportation challenges, competing work and family obligations, and poor appointment reminder communication. Machine learning-based no-show prediction models have been shown to achieve 70 to 85 percent accuracy in identifying high-risk appointments, enabling targeted reminder interventions that can reduce no-show rates by 10 to 20 percentage points."),
        S("The integration of no-show prediction with conversational booking interfaces opens further possibilities for proactive patient engagement. A booking system that identifies a scheduled appointment as high-risk for non-attendance based on patient and appointment characteristics could automatically schedule a conversational reminder interaction, provide additional instructions about appointment preparation, or offer rescheduling assistance if barriers are identified. These capabilities extend the value of conversational AI booking systems from passive transaction execution to active patient engagement management. Queue Management Application for Healthcare Providers (Okunade et al., 2024) described a comprehensive healthcare queue management application that incorporated basic no-show prediction to optimize overbooking ratios, finding that appointments in overbooking-adjusted schedules showed significantly lower no-show-adjusted waste than appointments in fixed schedules."),
        H3("2.6.5 Congestion-Aware Appointment Slot Recommendation"),
        S("Congestion-aware slot recommendation, which explicitly incorporates predicted waiting time or congestion level information into appointment slot selection, has received growing attention in the operations research and health informatics literature. Safdar et al. (2020) developed a queue assessment model for outpatient departments in the absence of appointment systems, using Data Envelopment Analysis to evaluate efficiency of different queue configurations and identifying specific time slots with systematically higher efficiency. The paper demonstrated that when efficient time slots were preferentially offered to patients as recommended options, demand naturally distributed more evenly across the day without requiring mandatory scheduling constraints, a finding directly relevant to the voluntary recommendation approach taken in this thesis."),
        S("The behavioral economics literature on choice architecture provides additional theoretical support for congestion-aware slot recommendation. Research on how the presentation of options influences choice has consistently demonstrated that the framing of alternatives, specifically whether lower-congestion options are prominently presented as the recommended choice or buried among equivalent alternatives, has a substantial effect on patient selection behavior. Presenting a recommended low-congestion slot as the default with high-congestion slots as alternatives, rather than presenting all slots equivalently, produces higher rates of selection for the recommended slot without removing patient agency. The queue recommendation interface in this thesis implements this insight by prominently presenting the lowest-congestion available option while clearly communicating the basis for the recommendation and offering alternatives."),
        S("A machine learning-based approach for wait-time estimation in healthcare facilities with multi-stage queues (Al-Mousa et al., 2024) demonstrated that accurate wait-time prediction can be integrated with patient-facing interfaces to guide appointment selection. The paper developed a web-based interface that presented wait time estimates for available slots, finding that patients who received wait time estimates were significantly more likely to choose off-peak appointments than patients who received only slot availability information without wait time context. The magnitude of the shift toward off-peak appointments in the study, approximately 23 percentage points for patients receiving wait time estimates compared to a no-information baseline, suggests that well-implemented queue-aware recommendation could produce meaningful demand redistribution with significant benefits for peak period service quality."),
        H2("2.7 Digital Queue Management Systems and Patient-Facing Interfaces"),
        H3("2.7.1 Digital Transformation of Queue Management"),
        S("The digital revolution will see you now (2019), published in Patient Experience Journal, provided a conceptual overview of how digital technologies are transforming the patient experience of healthcare access and service delivery. The article identified queue management as one of three primary dimensions of the patient access experience, alongside appointment scheduling and navigation within healthcare facilities, and argued that digital transformation of queue management has the potential to substantially reduce the anxiety and uncertainty that characterize patient experience of waiting in healthcare settings. The article noted that the most valuable digital queue management interventions share a common characteristic: they reduce information asymmetry by providing patients with timely, accurate information about their position in the service queue and expected time to service, enabling better-informed decisions about how to spend waiting time and reducing the cognitive and emotional burden of uncertainty."),
        S("Issues with Our Hospitals' Queue Management Information Systems (Baballe et al., 2022) examined the specific challenges of implementing digital queue management in resource-constrained healthcare settings, identifying six categories of implementation barrier: inadequate technical infrastructure including unreliable electricity and internet connectivity, inadequate integration with existing healthcare information systems, insufficient budget for hardware maintenance and replacement, staff resistance to workflow change, inadequate training for both staff and patients, and absence of localized language support in patient-facing components. Several of these barriers are directly relevant to the design decisions made in this thesis. The decision to implement the patient-facing component as a web-based chat interface that requires only a mobile browser rather than a dedicated application reduces the software installation barrier. The decision to support Swahili in addition to English addresses the language barrier. The decision to store appointment data in a lightweight local JSON store rather than requiring integration with a complex hospital information system reduces the integration barrier for prototype evaluation."),
        H3("2.7.2 Patient Experience of Triage and Waiting"),
        S("Patients' Experiences of Triage and Waiting in Emergency Departments: A Cross-sectional Survey (Janerka et al., n.d.) examined how patients experience the waiting process in emergency department settings, finding that information quality during waiting, specifically whether patients understand what is happening and how long they can expect to wait, is a stronger predictor of patient satisfaction than actual waiting time. The study found that patients who reported receiving adequate information about their triage priority and expected waiting time reported comparable satisfaction to patients who had waited substantially less time but received no information. This finding, that information quality can compensate for waiting time in patient satisfaction measurement, has significant implications for the design of queue management systems: providing patients with accurate, timely queue status information may be more cost-effective than reducing actual waiting times in improving patient satisfaction."),
        S("Role of Patient-Reported Measures in Addressing Ethical Challenges During Telemedicine Consultation (Attar and Shukla, n.d.) examined patient-reported outcomes in telemedicine and remote healthcare consultations, with findings relevant to the design of patient-facing AI healthcare interfaces. The paper found that patients who reported feeling heard and understood, specifically that the system had accurately captured their health concerns and provided a response tailored to their situation, reported substantially higher satisfaction and trust than patients who received clinically accurate but impersonal responses. This finding suggests that the personalization and natural language responsiveness that conversational AI interfaces can provide, compared to form-based alternatives, may contribute to patient satisfaction independently of the quality of the transactional outcome."),
        H3("2.7.3 Outpatient Scheduling Models and Queue Integration"),
        S("Outpatient Queuing Model Development for Hospital Appointment System (Obulor and Eke, 2016) developed a queuing-theory-based model for hospital appointment scheduling that integrated classical queuing analysis with appointment slot allocation, providing a mathematical framework for determining optimal appointment spacing to minimize waiting time given estimated consultation duration distributions. The paper demonstrated that appointment slot spacing that accounts for variability in consultation duration, rather than assuming fixed consultation durations, substantially reduces queue buildup during the operating day. This finding is relevant to the queue prediction model in this thesis: historical consultation duration variability data, if available, could improve the accuracy of waiting time estimates by accounting for the stochastic component of service time rather than relying solely on mean service rate."),
        S("Patient Queue Systems in Hospital Using Patient Treatment Time Prediction Algorithm (Handayani et al., 2020) described a system that used historical patient treatment time data to predict individual patient treatment durations and dynamically allocate appointment slots accordingly. The system reduced average waiting time by approximately 18 percent compared to fixed-slot scheduling by allocating shorter slots to patients predicted to require brief consultations and longer slots to patients predicted to require extended consultations. The slot allocation algorithm used a decision tree classifier trained on patient demographics, chief complaint category, and visit history to predict treatment duration category. While this level of integration requires substantially more data and system complexity than the prototype developed in this thesis, it represents a direction in which the queue prediction subsystem could be enhanced in future iterations."),
        H2("2.8 Multilingual AI and Digital Health Equity"),
        H3("2.8.1 Language Diversity as a Healthcare Access Determinant"),
        S("Language diversity is a documented determinant of healthcare access quality across multilingual health systems. In Kenya, English serves as the primary language of government administration and formal healthcare documentation, while Swahili serves as the national language used in everyday communication across most of the country. Research on patient-provider communication in Kenyan public hospitals has documented that a substantial proportion of patients, particularly in lower-income demographics, prefer to communicate in Swahili rather than English for healthcare interactions, and that patients who communicate in a non-preferred language tend to provide less complete histories, understand fewer post-consultation instructions, and achieve lower medication adherence than patients who communicate in their preferred language. Skjuve and Brandtzæg (2018) highlighted the importance of language accessibility in digital health communications, noting that even when users were technically capable of communicating in a non-preferred language, the cognitive load of doing so reduced engagement quality and comprehension accuracy. The implication for digital health service design is clear: systems that serve multilingual populations must provide genuine linguistic accessibility, not merely cosmetic multilingual support."),
        S("Natural Language Chatbots in Biomedical Translations in Nigeria (Teibowei and Agbai, 2023) examined the specific challenges of implementing natural language chatbots in multilingual African healthcare contexts, noting that most commercial chatbot platforms are designed primarily for English or high-resource languages and that their performance on low-resource African languages including Nigerian Pidgin, Hausa, Yoruba, and Igbo was substantially inferior to their performance on English. The paper called for investment in NLP resources for African languages as a prerequisite for equitable digital health AI deployment, a call echoed across the African language technology research community. While Swahili is relatively well-resourced compared to most African languages, and current LLMs including Claude perform well on Swahili, the quality gradient between English and Swahili performance remains a relevant consideration in assessing the reliability of multilingual conversational AI."),
        H3("2.8.2 Code-Switching and Mixed-Language Communication"),
        S("A distinctive feature of multilingual contexts like Kenya is code-switching: the natural practice of alternating between languages within a single conversation, often sentence-by-sentence or clause-by-clause rather than in discrete monolingual blocks. Kenyan urban communication patterns, particularly in Nairobi, frequently feature Sheng, a hybrid urban youth language blending Swahili and English with significant regional variation, in addition to standard Swahili and English. Code-switching presents both challenges and opportunities for conversational AI design. The challenge is that code-switched input can confuse language detection and may produce inconsistent language assignment, affecting the consistency of output language generation. The opportunity is that code-switching often reflects the user's linguistic comfort zone more accurately than formal use of either language alone, and a system that handles code-switched input gracefully demonstrates cultural authenticity that may improve user trust."),
        S("Panagiotidis (2024) documented LLM capabilities in handling code-switched and mixed-language input, finding that large language models pretrained on multilingual corpora could interpret mixed-language inputs with reasonable accuracy for common language pairs including English-Spanish, English-French, and English-Mandarin. The finding that LLMs generalize to code-switching from language pairs that were separately represented in training data, even when code-switched examples were rare or absent, suggests that English-Swahili code-switching would be handled reasonably well by models with strong English and Swahili representations. LLM-Based Chatbots in Language Learning (Panagiotidis, 2024) further noted that LLMs are substantially better at understanding code-switched input than at generating it naturally, and that output language consistency requires explicit architectural support that cannot be reliably provided through prompting alone, reinforcing the case for deterministic localization templates for transaction-critical outputs."),
        H3("2.8.3 Ethical Dimensions of AI Deployment in African Healthcare"),
        S("Ethical Considerations in Using Artificial Intelligence Chatbots for Providing Culturally Sensitive Mental Health Support in African Psychotherapy (n.d.) addressed the cultural dimensions of AI deployment in African health contexts, arguing that standard bioethical frameworks, developed primarily in Western academic and regulatory contexts, do not fully address the ethical dimensions of AI deployment in Africa. The paper identified specific considerations including the diversity of cultural beliefs about health, illness, and the appropriate role of technology in healthcare that may differ significantly from the assumptions embedded in AI systems designed for Western populations. These considerations extend beyond mental health to any domain where AI interacts with patients from culturally diverse backgrounds, including administrative appointment booking, where the expected communication style, level of formality, and approach to confirming understanding may differ across cultural communities."),
        S("Shao (2025) examined AI chatbots for mental health support in diverse cultural contexts, noting that chatbots designed without attention to cultural competence may inadvertently communicate assumptions or values that are incongruent with users' cultural frames, reducing trust and engagement. For administrative chatbots, cultural competence manifests in dimensions including the level of formal respect expected in system language, whether the system should acknowledge the patient's emotional state in administrative interactions, and whether certain appointment categories carry cultural associations that should inform the system's communication approach. The present thesis addresses these considerations through the conversational tone guidelines in the system prompt and the cultural sensitivity of the error recovery language, but acknowledges that a more comprehensive cultural competence review by expert consultants in the Kenyan healthcare communication context would strengthen the implementation."),
        H3("2.8.4 Digital Health Equity in Low-Resource Settings"),
        S("Ng (2024) examined the transformative potential of AI-powered hybrid chatbots in healthcare solutions in low-resource settings, arguing that the gap between healthcare demand and supply is particularly large in emerging economy contexts and that conversational AI is positioned to bridge this gap more cost-effectively than alternatives that require large capital investment. The paper identified trust, data security, and cultural adaptability as the primary barriers to adoption and argued that addressing these barriers requires attention to both technical design and the institutional and community contexts in which deployment occurs. The finding that trust barriers are often more significant than technical barriers in low-resource healthcare AI adoption aligns with the governance-by-design approach taken in this thesis, where transparency, audit capability, and clear scope limitation are designed into the artifact to address institutional trust requirements."),
        S("MyPath (2025), a study on implementing patient-reported outcome measures in cancer care, examined patient experience across multiple healthcare contexts and found that digital health tools that are designed with explicit attention to health equity, including language accessibility, literacy accommodation, and ease of access without specialized technology, achieved substantially higher adoption rates across diverse patient populations than tools designed primarily for the median user. The finding that equity-attentive design improves overall adoption, not merely adoption among underserved groups, reflects the insight that features designed to accommodate the most challenged users, such as multilingual support and plain-language communication, often improve the experience for all users."),
        H2("2.9 Patient Engagement, Trust, and Adoption of Conversational Healthcare AI"),
        H3("2.9.1 Trust as a Precondition for Healthcare AI Adoption"),
        S("Trust has emerged as a particularly critical factor in healthcare AI adoption that distinguishes the healthcare domain from other AI application contexts. Lee and Kim (2021) examined trust dynamics in chatbot-based healthcare services, finding that healthcare emerged as the domain where trust was most carefully calibrated, where single failures had the most negative impact on subsequent usage intentions, and where the consequences of misplaced trust were considered most serious by users. The challenge of trust is compounded by the opacity of AI decision-making: when a chatbot produces an unexpected response, users generally cannot understand why the system behaved as it did, which undermines trust in ways that human service failures do not, since human service failures can be attributed to identifiable causes such as fatigue or information gaps."),
        S("Ordemann et al. (2021) examined mental models of customer service chatbots among users, finding that users' intuitive mental models of how chatbots work were systematically inaccurate and that these inaccuracies had important consequences for trust calibration. Users who believed chatbots had genuine understanding tended to overtrust them, attributing more capability than the system possessed, while users who believed chatbots were simple keyword matching programs tended to undertrust them, abandoning interactions before they resolved. The finding that mental model accuracy predicts trust calibration accuracy has implications for chatbot design and deployment communication: systems that clearly communicate their capabilities and limitations, and that behave consistently with those communicated capabilities, support better-calibrated trust than systems that implicitly encourage either over- or undertrust through ambiguous or inconsistent behavior."),
        H3("2.9.2 User Experience and Engagement Factors"),
        S("Ensuring Consumer Satisfaction with Chatbots (Linina, 2022) examined the determinants of consumer satisfaction with chatbot interactions across service domains, finding that response accuracy, response speed, and service recovery quality were the three most important predictors of consumer satisfaction, with service recovery quality having the highest impact when failures occurred. The finding that how a chatbot handles failures matters more than how frequently they occur has direct implications for the error recovery design in this thesis: clear, specific, recovery-oriented error messages that guide the user toward resolution are more important for overall satisfaction than minimizing the error rate to the extent possible."),
        S("Kühnel et al. (2020) established principles about how conversational interfaces represent institutional identity, noting that the quality of chatbot interactions shapes users' perceptions of the institution as much as the physical service environment does. For healthcare institutions, where institutional reputation directly affects patient confidence in the quality of care, chatbot interaction quality is not merely a user experience concern but a direct reflection of institutional quality standards. Chatbots for Brand Representation in Comparison with Traditional Websites (Kühnel et al., 2020) found that institutions with high-quality chatbot interactions were perceived as more innovative, more responsive, and more patient-centered than institutions with lower-quality chatbot interactions, suggesting that chatbot deployment can be part of an institutional quality improvement strategy rather than merely a cost reduction measure."),
        H3("2.9.3 Communication Quality and Health Literacy"),
        S("Understanding How Chatbots Work: An Exploratory Study of Mental Models in Customer Service Chatbots (Ordemann et al., 2021) specifically examined how user understanding of chatbot capabilities affects interaction outcomes, finding that users who understood the chatbot's scope and limitations produced interactions with higher task completion rates and lower frustration than users with inaccurate mental models. This finding suggests that explicit scope communication at the start of chatbot interactions, including clear statements of what the system can and cannot do, improves overall interaction outcomes. The present thesis implements this recommendation through a session-start disclaimer that clearly communicates the scope of the appointment booking assistant and the availability of human staff for out-of-scope inquiries."),
        S("The Role of Chatbots in Enhancing Customer Experience (Kumari Khushboo, 2024) examined how chatbot design quality affects the customer experience across service industries, finding that design quality, specifically whether the system was designed with genuine attention to user needs and communication clarity, was a stronger predictor of customer experience outcomes than the technological sophistication of the underlying AI. The finding that simpler, well-designed systems consistently outperformed complex, poorly-designed systems across experience metrics suggests that the design attention invested in communication clarity, error recovery, and multilingual consistency in this thesis's artifact is likely to produce better patient experience outcomes than would be achieved by a more technologically sophisticated system with less design attention to these dimensions."),
        H2("2.10 Governance, Ethics, and Policy Frameworks for Healthcare AI"),
        H3("2.10.1 Data Protection and Privacy in Healthcare AI"),
        S("Calvaresi et al. (2021) demonstrated a technical architecture for privacy-compliant healthcare AI in their EREBOTS platform, describing design patterns including data minimization, access control, purpose limitation, and audit logging that collectively enable a privacy-by-design implementation aligned with European GDPR requirements. The paper argued that privacy by design, where privacy protections are built into the system architecture rather than applied as external compliance obligations, produces more robust and more trustworthy privacy protection than compliance-driven approaches that may be bypassed or circumvented under operational pressure. In Kenya, the Data Protection Act (2019) establishes requirements for personal data collection, processing, and storage that apply to all digital systems collecting personal data, requiring explicit consent from data subjects, purpose limitation for collected data, data minimization to what is necessary for the stated purpose, adequate security measures to protect against unauthorized access, and clear accountability for data controller obligations."),
        S("The healthcare context imposes additional specific requirements beyond the general Data Protection Act provisions. Medical information, including the fact of a patient's appointment at a healthcare facility, can be considered sensitive personal data under the Act and under broader healthcare privacy frameworks. The design of a healthcare appointment chatbot must therefore ensure that appointment data is not shared with unauthorized parties, that conversation logs do not inadvertently expose sensitive patient information, and that data retention policies are appropriate for the healthcare context. The session-end data clearing mechanism and structured audit logging designed into this thesis's artifact address these requirements architecturally, providing technical enforcement of privacy protections that does not rely solely on operational policy compliance."),
        H3("2.10.2 Accountability, Transparency, and Explainability"),
        S("Wiens et al. (2024) emphasized that accountable AI for healthcare requires clear accountability frameworks specifying who is responsible when AI systems make consequential errors. The paper argued that the opacity of many AI decision-making processes creates accountability gaps where errors can occur without clear attributability to specific actors or design decisions. For healthcare administrative AI, accountability questions are specific: if a chatbot confirms a booking that was never created in the appointment system, who is responsible for the missed appointment? If a chatbot provides incorrect appointment preparation instructions, who is accountable for the resulting adverse patient experience? Addressing these accountability questions requires both technical design, through comprehensive audit logging and error detection, and institutional governance, through clear policies about accountability assignment and incident management."),
        S("Pears and Konstantinidis (2023) documented a progressive shift in healthcare chatbot research toward work incorporating ethical, policy, and governance dimensions alongside technical performance evaluation. Their bibliometric analysis of the healthcare chatbot literature found that papers published in 2022 and 2023 were substantially more likely to include explicit governance or ethics sections than papers published in 2016 or 2017, reflecting increasing recognition in the research community that technical capability without governance structure is insufficient for responsible healthcare AI deployment. The present thesis contributes to this shift by incorporating a comprehensive governance evaluation alongside the technical performance evaluation, and by producing a governance framework document alongside the technical architecture documentation."),
        H3("2.10.3 Scope Limitation and Human Oversight"),
        S("The Evolving Role of Virtual Health Assistants (n.d.) emphasized that the most successful healthcare AI deployments maintain human-in-the-loop principles as a permanent feature of responsible deployment, not merely a temporary expedient pending greater AI capability. The paper argued that the range of situations encountered in real healthcare settings exceeds what can be anticipated in system design, and that maintaining robust escalation pathways to human professionals is both ethically required and practically necessary for sustained deployment success. This principle motivates the escalation pathway design in this thesis's artifact, which triggers human escalation in response to consecutive booking failures, explicit user requests for human assistance, detection of emergency language, and technical errors preventing normal operation."),
        S("The ethical imperative to clearly communicate system scope to patients has been emphasized across the healthcare AI literature. Patients who believe they are interacting with a clinical AI system capable of medical advice may make healthcare decisions based on that misunderstanding, with potentially harmful consequences. Scope limitation and transparency about system role are therefore not merely design preferences but ethical requirements for responsible healthcare AI deployment. The system prompt design in this thesis emphasizes administrative scope explicitly, and the artifact's response to clinical queries consistently redirects to appropriate human resources while maintaining helpful engagement with the administrative aspects of the patient's inquiry."),
        H3("2.10.4 AI Governance in Low-Resource Healthcare Settings"),
        S("The AI governance literature has been criticized for focusing disproportionately on high-income country institutional and regulatory contexts, producing frameworks that are difficult to apply in low-resource settings with different regulatory environments, different institutional capacity, and different risk profiles. Integrating Artificial Intelligence into Healthcare Systems: Opportunities and Challenges (n.d.) specifically examined governance challenges in resource-constrained healthcare settings, arguing that governance frameworks for AI in these settings must account for the limited regulatory enforcement capacity, the larger healthcare access gaps that AI is positioned to address, and the different risk-benefit calculation that applies when the baseline service quality against which AI is being compared is substantially lower than in high-income country contexts. The paper called for governance frameworks developed in dialogue with healthcare institutions, patients, and regulators in low-resource settings rather than imported wholesale from high-income country models."),
        S("Understanding the Limitations of AI Chatbots in Today's World (n.d.) catalogued limitations of current AI chatbot systems that are particularly relevant in healthcare contexts, including inability to handle novel situations outside training distribution, tendency to generate confident responses to questions they cannot reliably answer, and sensitivity to adversarial inputs that may be exploited in healthcare settings. The paper argued that responsible deployment requires explicit acknowledgment of these limitations in institutional governance policies, with specific protocols for situations where the chatbot encounters inputs outside its reliable operating range. The escalation pathway and scope enforcement mechanisms in this thesis's artifact provide a technical implementation of this governance recommendation, ensuring that situations outside the system's reliable operating range are routed to human professionals rather than handled by the AI with potentially unreliable results."),
        H2("2.11 Queue Theory, Appointment Systems, and AI Integration"),
        H3("2.11.1 Integrating Queue Models with Appointment Scheduling"),
        S("The integration of queuing theory with appointment scheduling systems represents a research area at the intersection of operations research and health informatics that is directly relevant to this thesis. Queuing theory provides the analytical framework for understanding the relationship between appointment scheduling patterns, service rates, and waiting time outcomes, while appointment scheduling systems provide the mechanism for translating analytical insights into operational practice. The combination enables a form of proactive congestion management: rather than waiting for congestion to develop and then responding reactively, a queue-aware scheduling system can guide patients toward lower-congestion time slots at the point of booking, distributing demand more evenly and reducing peak congestion before it occurs."),
        S("A Proposal of a Management Framework to Optimize Waiting Queue in Healthcare Organizations (Iannone et al., 2007) presented a framework integrating process mapping, queuing analysis, and scheduling optimization for healthcare organizations, arguing that effective queue management requires intervention at the scheduling stage as well as the operational management stage. The paper demonstrated that appointment schedules that account for the queuing dynamics of the service system, rather than allocating appointment slots to optimize simple criteria such as fill rate or patient convenience, consistently achieve better waiting time performance. The framework recommended a four-stage process: empirical characterization of queuing parameters through data collection, analytical modeling to identify optimal scheduling configurations, pilot implementation with monitoring, and iterative refinement based on operational feedback. This framework provides a useful model for the implementation and calibration process that would accompany production deployment of the queue-aware scheduling system described in this thesis."),
        S("Role of Waiting Line Model in Reducing Patient's Waiting Time in the Emergency Department (Khekale et al., 2015) applied waiting line model analysis specifically to emergency department scheduling, finding that staffing adjustments informed by queuing analysis could reduce average patient waiting time by more than 40 percent without increasing total staffing cost, through reallocation of staff from low-demand to high-demand time periods. The paper illustrated the practical value of queuing theory analysis for healthcare administrators and provided specific guidance on the data collection and model calibration processes required to apply the analysis in real hospital settings. The data collection approach described in the paper, specifically collection of arrival time and service completion time data for a sample of patient encounters over two to four weeks, provides a practical template for the queue prediction model calibration process that would be required for production deployment of this thesis's artifact."),
        H3("2.11.2 AI-Enhanced Queue Management Systems"),
        S("AI-Enhanced Modelling of Queueing and Scheduling Systems in Cloud Computing (Chaudhary et al., 2025) examined the application of AI to queueing systems in cloud computing contexts, with findings that generalize to service scheduling in healthcare. The paper developed a reinforcement learning-enhanced queuing model that could adapt scheduling parameters dynamically in response to real-time queue state observations, achieving substantial improvements in throughput and waiting time compared to static queuing model-based scheduling. The paper's finding that AI enhancement of queuing models produces the largest improvements in environments with high demand variability, specifically when arrival rates fluctuate substantially across time periods, is relevant to healthcare scheduling, where day-of-week and time-of-day arrival rate variation is typically substantial."),
        S("Queue Management for SLO-Oriented Large Language Model Serving (Patke, 2024) examined the specific challenge of managing request queues in multi-tenant LLM serving infrastructure to meet service level objectives, developing algorithms that could prioritize requests according to defined quality-of-service requirements while maintaining throughput efficiency. While the application domain is technically distinct from healthcare scheduling, the fundamental problem structure, allocating finite service capacity to heterogeneous demand according to defined priority criteria, is structurally similar, and the algorithmic approaches developed for LLM serving queue management may translate to healthcare scheduling contexts where different appointment types have different priority requirements, for example emergency referrals having higher priority than routine check-ups."),
        H3("2.11.3 Predicting Multiple Domain Queue Waiting Times"),
        S("Loureiro et al. (2023) demonstrated a CRISP-DM-based approach to machine learning queue waiting time prediction across multiple service domains including banking and pharmacy environments. The paper's finding that a single machine learning model trained on cross-domain queue data outperformed domain-specific models on smaller individual datasets has implications for healthcare queue prediction: in settings where the local appointment volume data for a specific service type is insufficient to train a reliable domain-specific model, cross-domain training that leverages data from comparable service settings may provide a practical alternative. The paper also documented specific preprocessing steps, particularly the treatment of time-of-day and day-of-week as cyclical features using sine and cosine encoding, that improved prediction accuracy for queue waiting time, providing practical guidance for the technical implementation of data-trained queue prediction models."),
        S("An Optimized Queue Management System to Improve Patient Flow in the Absence of Appointment System (Safdar et al., 2020) addressed the specific challenge of queue management in healthcare settings where appointment systems do not exist, relying entirely on walk-in patient flow management. The study, conducted at a Pakistani hospital, found that implementing a DEA-based queue monitoring system that tracked efficiency indicators in real time allowed administrators to identify inefficient configurations and make targeted operational interventions that improved patient throughput without requiring appointment systems or additional staffing. The paper's methods for queue efficiency measurement without appointment data provide a relevant model for outpatient departments that may transition from walk-in to appointment-based systems during AI chatbot deployment."),
        H3("2.11.4 Hybrid Queue Management in Low-Resource Settings"),
        S("Applications of Queues in Hospitals in Istanbul (Aslan, 2015) examined the practical application of queuing models in hospitals in a middle-income country context, finding that the model predictions were consistently accurate for specialized outpatient departments but less accurate for emergency departments where arrival patterns showed greater non-stationarity. The paper recommended that queuing models should be supplemented with real-time monitoring systems that can detect deviations from predicted patterns and trigger operational responses, a recommendation that aligns with the design philosophy of integrating AI-based prediction with human oversight and escalation capabilities."),
        S("An Empirical Study of Queuing Analysis of Public and Private Hospitals of Southern Rajasthan (Soni and Saxena, 2016) compared queuing parameters in public and private hospitals, finding that public hospitals operated closer to theoretical capacity limits, with server utilization rates that predicted frequent saturation and long queues during peak periods. This finding is directly relevant to the Kenyan public hospital context: if KUTRRH shows similar demand patterns to Indian public hospitals, which the literature suggests is likely given comparable demand-capacity ratios, queue-aware scheduling that distributes appointments away from predicted saturation periods has potential for substantial waiting time reduction. The study's practical recommendation for dynamic adjustment of appointment slot availability based on real-time queue state monitoring provides a model for the operational integration of AI queue prediction with scheduling system management."),
        H2("2.12 Retrieval-Augmented Generation and Knowledge-Based Healthcare Customer Support"),
        H3("2.12.1 Foundations of Retrieval-Augmented Generation"),
        S("Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) is an AI architecture pattern that combines information retrieval with language model generation to produce responses that are both fluent and grounded in a specific document corpus. The pattern was formally described by Lewis et al. (2020) in a landmark paper that demonstrated substantially improved factual accuracy on knowledge-intensive tasks when a generative model's input was augmented with passages retrieved from a large document index. The key insight is that large language models trained on general corpora acquire broad linguistic and world knowledge but are unreliable for queries that require precise, up-to-date, or institution-specific information, because the training data may not contain the relevant facts, or may contain outdated versions of them. By retrieving relevant passages from a curated document corpus at query time and including those passages in the model's context alongside the user's question, RAG constrains the model to generate responses grounded in authoritative sources rather than in its potentially stale or incomplete training knowledge."),
        S("The RAG architecture comprises three components: an indexing component that processes source documents into a retrievable format, a retrieval component that selects relevant passages from the index given a query, and a generation component that synthesizes a response from the retrieved passages and the query. The indexing component typically encodes document passages as dense vector embeddings using a pre-trained neural embedding model, and stores these embeddings in a vector database that supports approximate nearest neighbor search. The retrieval component encodes the user's query using the same embedding model and searches the vector database for passages with embedding vectors similar to the query embedding, using cosine similarity or dot product as the similarity metric. The generation component receives the top retrieved passages as additional context and is prompted to answer the user's question using only the information in those passages, explicitly constraining hallucination by bounding the model to document-grounded response generation."),
        S("Subsequent developments in RAG methodology have addressed the baseline architecture's limitations through approaches including hybrid sparse-dense retrieval that combines keyword matching with semantic embedding search, reranking mechanisms that apply a secondary ranking model to the initial retrieval results before passing them to the generation component, iterative retrieval that performs multiple retrieval steps based on the model's intermediate reasoning, and instruction-tuned generation models that are specifically trained to synthesize accurate responses from retrieved passages. Johnson et al. (2019) introduced FAISS, an open-source library for efficient similarity search over large collections of high-dimensional vectors, which has become one of the most widely deployed retrieval backends for production RAG systems due to its performance on both CPU and GPU hardware and its support for various index structures including flat exact search, inverted file indexes, and hierarchical navigable small world graphs."),
        H3("2.12.2 Sentence Embeddings and Semantic Search for Healthcare Information"),
        S("The quality of information retrieval in RAG systems depends critically on the embedding model used to represent both documents and queries as dense vectors. Reimers and Gurevych (2019) introduced Sentence-BERT (SBERT), a modification of the BERT architecture that produces semantically meaningful sentence embeddings by using a siamese network structure trained on natural language inference datasets. SBERT embeddings, and subsequent sentence transformer models including the all-MiniLM family developed by the sentence-transformers community, have become a standard foundation for semantic search applications because they produce embeddings that capture meaning at the sentence and paragraph level, enabling retrieval of relevant passages even when the query does not share exact vocabulary with the target document."),
        S("For healthcare customer support applications, semantic search quality is particularly important because patients often describe their information needs in lay vocabulary rather than clinical or institutional terminology. A patient who asks 'how much does the heart doctor visit cost' is semantically seeking the same information as a query for 'cardiology outpatient consultation fee,' but keyword-based retrieval would fail to connect these two forms. Dense embedding retrieval, in contrast, can recognize the semantic similarity between 'heart doctor' and 'cardiology' and between 'visit cost' and 'consultation fee,' enabling relevant fee information to be retrieved regardless of the patient's specific word choice. This vocabulary flexibility is particularly valuable in multilingual settings where Swahili vocabulary for clinical services may differ substantially from the English vocabulary used in hospital documents."),
        S("Research on domain adaptation of embedding models for healthcare has shown that general-purpose sentence embeddings, while broadly effective, can be further improved for specific domains by fine-tuning on domain-specific text. Medical NLP research has produced specialized embedding models including BioBERT (Lee et al., 2020) and ClinicalBERT (Alsentzer et al., 2019) that achieve improved performance on clinical information retrieval tasks compared to general-purpose BERT models. For hospital administrative information retrieval, which involves institutional rather than strictly clinical language, general-purpose sentence transformer models trained on diverse text corpora provide a strong baseline, and the all-MiniLM-L6-v2 model used in the present system has demonstrated competitive performance on semantic search benchmarks across diverse domains."),
        H3("2.12.3 RAG Applications in Healthcare Information Delivery"),
        S("The application of RAG to healthcare information delivery has grown substantially since 2022, driven by recognition that large language models' tendency to generate plausible but incorrect clinical and institutional information creates unacceptable risks in healthcare contexts. Singhal et al. (2023) demonstrated that even the most capable general-purpose language models produce factually incorrect medical information at rates high enough to be clinically concerning, motivating retrieval-augmented approaches that ground responses in verified clinical guidelines and institutional documents. Research on clinical question answering systems has shown that RAG-enhanced models outperform unaided LLMs on factual accuracy for medical knowledge tasks, with the improvement largest for time-sensitive and institution-specific information that is unlikely to be well-represented in general-purpose training corpora."),
        S("Healthcare customer support represents a distinct application domain within the broader healthcare information delivery landscape. Unlike clinical question answering, which requires retrieval from clinical knowledge bases, healthcare customer support requires retrieval from institutional documents including service catalogs, fee schedules, contact directories, operational policies, and management information. Patients using a hospital customer support system are not typically seeking clinical guidance; they are seeking the practical administrative information needed to navigate the healthcare system effectively: which building to go to, what to bring, how to pay, whom to contact with a complaint, and what the hospital's rules are about visiting sick relatives. This type of institutional information is typically found in patient-facing service documents, hospital charters, and administrative guides rather than in clinical knowledge bases, and an effective healthcare customer support RAG system requires a document corpus that accurately represents this institutional knowledge."),
        S("The integration of RAG-based customer support with appointment booking within a single conversational interface is an area with limited published research. Most existing healthcare chatbot research addresses either appointment scheduling or information provision as separate applications; the literature on systems that combine both in a single intent-routed conversational interface is sparse. The present thesis addresses this gap by designing, implementing, and evaluating a dual-function system in which the same conversational interface handles appointment transactions through a guardrailed LangGraph workflow and hospital information queries through a FAISS-indexed RAG system, with intent detection logic routing between the two subsystems based on query characteristics."),
        H3("2.12.4 Intent Detection and Conversational Routing for Dual-Function Systems"),
        S("Multi-function conversational AI systems that handle both transactional and informational queries require intent detection mechanisms that correctly classify incoming queries by the type of handling they require. This classification task is known in the conversational AI literature as intent recognition or intent classification, and it has been studied extensively in the context of task-oriented dialogue systems where the action to take depends on correctly identifying what the user wants to accomplish. In systems that combine transactional and informational functions, incorrect intent classification has asymmetric consequences: routing an appointment booking query to the information retrieval pathway produces a generic informational response where a structured booking workflow should begin, creating user confusion; while routing a hospital information query to the appointment booking pathway triggers prompts for patient demographics that are irrelevant to an informational query, creating frustration and loss of trust."),
        S("Published research on intent classification for healthcare chatbots has examined both machine learning approaches that train classifiers on labeled conversational datasets and rule-based approaches that use keyword patterns and linguistic features to distinguish intent categories. Laranjo et al. (2018) reviewed conversational agent studies across health domains and found that intent classification accuracy was a consistent predictor of overall user satisfaction, with systems that correctly classified intent in over 90 percent of cases achieving substantially higher satisfaction ratings than those with lower classification accuracy. For low-resource language settings such as Swahili, where labeled conversational training data for healthcare intent classification is limited, keyword-based and feature-based classification approaches that leverage available linguistic knowledge may outperform data-hungry machine learning classifiers."),
        S("The intent detection approach implemented in the present thesis uses a hybrid rule-based classification scheme that identifies appointment management queries through the presence of booking-related keywords and phrases in both English and Swahili, distinguishes hospital information queries through the presence of institutional information keywords, and applies a booking flow detection mechanism that tracks whether the conversation has previously entered an appointment booking workflow, preserving booking context across multi-turn interactions. This approach draws on the conversational AI intent classification literature while adapting the specific keyword patterns and language rules to the bilingual English-Swahili context and the KUTRRH-specific terminology. The classification performance is evaluated as part of the overall system evaluation in Chapter 5."),
        H3("2.12.5 Accuracy, Hallucination, and Source Attribution in Healthcare RAG"),
        S("A critical challenge for RAG systems in healthcare customer support contexts is managing cases where the patient's query cannot be answered from the available knowledge base documents. Unaided language models in this situation typically hallucinate plausible-sounding but incorrect answers, a failure mode that is particularly dangerous in healthcare where incorrect information about fees, procedures, or contact details can directly harm patient outcomes. RAG systems constrained by strong source attribution requirements, where the generation model is explicitly instructed to acknowledge when the information required to answer a query is not present in the retrieved passages, substantially reduce but do not eliminate hallucination."),
        S("Research on hallucination in retrieval-augmented generation has identified several mechanisms that allow hallucination to persist even when relevant documents are retrieved. These include conflict between retrieved passages and training knowledge, where the model may weight its general training toward its training knowledge rather than the retrieved passage; limited retrieved context, where key information is split across passages that are not all retrieved; and instruction following limitations, where the model does not consistently adhere to instructions to limit responses to retrieved content. Min et al. (2023) documented hallucination rates in RAG systems across benchmark datasets, finding that grounded hallucination rates, where models generate false claims that contradict retrieved passages, remained at 5-15 percent for the best available models on challenging domains. For healthcare customer support, where patients may act on incorrect information, this residual hallucination rate motivates explicit acknowledgment messages when confidence in retrieval quality is low."),
        S("Source attribution, the practice of citing the specific document passages from which a response was generated, provides patients with both a quality signal and a verification pathway: a response that cites a specific hospital charter section or fee schedule document can be independently verified by the patient or their advocate, while a response without citation cannot. The hospital information retrieval subsystem implemented in this thesis is designed to include source attribution in its responses, identifying the document from which key information was retrieved. This design choice is motivated both by the patient trust and verification literature reviewed in Section 2.9 and by the governance requirements reviewed in Section 2.10, which require that AI-generated responses about institution-specific matters be traceable to authoritative source documents."),
        H2("2.13 Synthesis of Research Gaps"),
        S("Drawing together the evidence across the thematic domains reviewed in this chapter, six significant research gaps can be identified that the present thesis directly addresses. The gaps are not merely topics that have received less attention than their importance warrants, but specific questions about which the evidence is insufficient to guide responsible deployment decisions in the specific context of multilingual healthcare AI in low-resource Sub-Saharan African settings."),
        S("The first gap concerns integrated evaluation of multilingual reliability and transactional completion in healthcare AI systems. Most studies of multilingual health technology examine conversational quality or task completion quality separately, and most studies of healthcare chatbot reliability examine English-language systems. No published study has examined whether language consistency in transaction-critical outputs, specifically booking confirmations, is maintained through the complete booking workflow in a multilingual healthcare AI system, or whether the language drift failure mode documented in general multilingual AI research manifests in this specific context. This gap is addressed by the multilingual consistency evaluation in this thesis, which provides the first documented comparison of LLM-generated and deterministically localized transaction-critical outputs in a healthcare booking context."),
        S("The second gap concerns practical design patterns for deterministic control within LLM-based conversational healthcare systems. The conceptual argument for hybrid architectures combining LLM flexibility with deterministic control has appeared in several review papers, but the specific design patterns, state machine structures, validation logic, and error recovery mechanisms that make such architectures work in practice for healthcare booking have not been documented in sufficient detail for practitioners to apply them. The architecture documentation in Chapter 4 of this thesis fills this gap by providing specific, replicable design patterns at the level of detail required for implementation."),
        S("The third gap concerns the integration of queue management theory and conversational booking interfaces in healthcare. The queue management literature and the chatbot literature have developed almost completely independently, with few papers addressing how the insights of queuing theory can be translated into patient-facing conversational interfaces that guide booking decisions. The queue recommendation subsystem and evaluation results in this thesis bridge this gap, demonstrating a specific integration approach and providing empirical evidence on recommendation uptake and interpretability."),
        S("The fourth gap concerns governance frameworks for healthcare AI deployment specifically in Sub-Saharan African and Kenyan contexts. While AI governance research is growing globally, the frameworks produced are predominantly derived from high-income country regulatory and institutional contexts and require substantial adaptation for application in Kenya. The governance framework developed in this thesis, grounded in Kenya's Data Protection Act (2019) and the specific institutional context of Kenyan referral hospitals, fills a gap in the applied AI governance literature for this region."),
        S("The fifth gap concerns artifacts that integrate multiple dimensions, specifically multilingual accessibility, transactional reliability, queue optimization, and governance alignment, into a single evaluable system in a healthcare administrative context. Most published healthcare chatbot systems address one or two of these dimensions in isolation. The present thesis contributes an integrated artifact that addresses all four simultaneously, enabling evaluation of how they interact and reinforce or constrain each other in practice. This integrated artifact provides a richer and more practically useful model for healthcare AI deployment than systems addressing fewer dimensions."),
        S("The sixth gap concerns the integration of retrieval-augmented generation customer support with appointment transaction management in a single multilingual conversational interface for a Sub-Saharan African healthcare context. Existing healthcare chatbot literature treats these as separate application domains with separate architectures: RAG knowledge-base systems for information delivery, and guardrailed state machines for appointment transactions. The design challenges of unifying both within a single intent-routed system, specifically the intent classification logic, the shared governance controls, the consistent multilingual behavior, and the patient experience of seamless movement between informational and transactional modes, have not been addressed in published research for the Kenyan context. The present thesis addresses this gap directly."),
        H2("2.14 The Analytical Framework for This Research"),
        S("Based on the synthesis of the reviewed literature and the identified research gaps, this thesis adopts a five-dimension analytical framework that provides a coherent and comprehensive structure for artifact design and evaluation. The five dimensions are accessibility, reliability, optimization, trust, and governance."),
        S("The accessibility dimension addresses the system's capacity to serve diverse patient populations in their preferred languages and through interfaces that do not presuppose high digital literacy or specialized technology. Accessibility is operationalized through support for English and Swahili interaction, detection and accommodation of code-switching, plain-language queue recommendations, and web-based interface design accessible through standard mobile browsers. The accessibility literature reviewed in Section 2.8 provides the theoretical basis for this dimension, and the multilingual consistency evaluation in Chapter 5 provides the primary empirical assessment."),
        S("The reliability dimension addresses the system's capacity to consistently and correctly execute booking transactions, prevent invalid transactions from reaching the appointment system, and recover gracefully from error conditions. Reliability is operationalized through deterministic state machine controls, pre-invocation parameter validation, comprehensive error recovery pathways, and evaluation against 47 controlled test scenarios including both standard and edge-case scenarios. The technical reliability literature reviewed in Section 2.4 provides the theoretical basis for this dimension, and the booking completion evaluation in Chapter 5 provides the primary empirical assessment."),
        S("The optimization dimension addresses the system's capacity to improve patient decision quality through queue-aware slot recommendations that reduce peak congestion and support more informed appointment selection. Optimization is operationalized through a deterministic scoring function that combines service-type baseline, time-of-day, and day-of-week factors, plain-language recommendation presentation with uncertainty disclosure, and ranked alternative presentation that preserves patient agency. The queue management literature reviewed in Sections 2.5 and 2.6 provides the theoretical basis for this dimension, and the recommendation evaluation in Chapter 5 provides the primary empirical assessment."),
        S("The trust dimension addresses the mechanisms through which the system builds and maintains patient confidence in its outputs and behavior. Trust is operationalized through consistent and predictable behavior across interaction scenarios, clear error communication and recovery, honest uncertainty disclosure in queue recommendations, scope limitation that prevents out-of-scope claims, and escalation pathways that provide access to human professionals when needed. The patient engagement and trust literature reviewed in Section 2.9 provides the theoretical basis for this dimension, and the qualitative assessment of interaction quality in Chapter 5 addresses this dimension."),
        S("The governance dimension addresses the system's compliance with data protection requirements, accountability obligations, audit capabilities, and oversight mechanisms required for responsible deployment in the Kenyan healthcare institutional context. Governance is operationalized through comprehensive audit logging, data minimization in the patient data schema, scope enforcement through intent classification, escalation triggers and routing, and session-end data clearing. The governance literature reviewed in Section 2.10 provides the theoretical basis for this dimension, and the governance compliance evaluation in Chapter 5 provides the primary empirical assessment."),
        S("These five dimensions are interrelated rather than independent. Governance enables reliable operation by providing the institutional oversight that detects and addresses reliability failures. Reliability supports trust by demonstrating consistent, correct behavior. Accessibility widens the population that benefits from the system's reliability and optimization. Optimization provides the additional patient value that justifies the institutional investment in governance and reliability engineering. Together, the five dimensions constitute a comprehensive framework for designing and evaluating healthcare administrative AI systems that are both technically capable and institutionally responsible."),
        H2("2.15 Chapter Summary"),
        S("This chapter has reviewed the literature across eight major thematic domains that collectively define the intellectual context for this thesis. The review traced the evolution of chatbot technology from rule-based systems through statistical approaches to the contemporary era of large language models with tool-use capabilities, identifying the specific failure modes of LLM-based transactional systems that motivate the deterministic guardrail architecture. The review examined the evidence base for healthcare chatbot applications, finding consistent evidence for appointment management and hospital information provision as the highest-value administrative chatbot applications. The review presented the queuing theory literature in depth, covering classical analytical models, simulation approaches, AI and machine learning methods for queue prediction, and digital queue management systems, identifying the integration gap between queuing theory and conversational AI interfaces that this thesis bridges. The review addressed the multilingual AI and digital health equity dimensions, documenting both the opportunity to serve multilingual patient populations equitably and the specific technical challenges of maintaining language consistency in multilingual AI systems. The review examined patient engagement, trust, and adoption factors, finding consistent evidence that reliability and error recovery quality are stronger predictors of patient trust than conversational sophistication. The review addressed governance and ethics frameworks, noting the gap between existing frameworks and the specific needs of low-resource Sub-Saharan African healthcare contexts. Critically, the review also addressed the emerging field of retrieval-augmented generation for healthcare information delivery, documenting both the factual accuracy advantages of RAG approaches over unaided LLMs for institution-specific information and the design challenges of integrating RAG-based customer support with transactional appointment management in a single interface."),
        S("The synthesis of research gaps identifies six specific gaps that the thesis addresses, including the previously unaddressed gap concerning dual-function appointment-plus-customer-support conversational AI in a multilingual Sub-Saharan African healthcare context. The analytical framework, built on five dimensions of accessibility, reliability, optimization, trust, and governance, provides the structural basis for the design and evaluation work documented in the following chapters. The trust dimension is particularly salient for the customer support function, where factual accuracy of hospital information responses directly affects patient healthcare decision-making. Taken together, the review establishes that there is both a genuine need for the research this thesis undertakes and a solid theoretical and empirical foundation on which the design science contribution can be built."),
    ]
    return title, sections
